.TH CSORT_SORT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
csort_sort \- sort any collection with custom getter and comparison
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/csort.h>

bool sorted = csort_sort(collection, length, elem_size, getter_proc,
                         comparison_proc, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This generic sort macro sorts any collection with an iterative mergesort
algorithm. It works with any type of collection: an array, a vector or a
custom container. The caller must give a getter function and a comparison
function.

.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
collection      Pointer to the collection to sort
length          Number of elements in the collection
elem_size       Size of each element in bytes
getter_proc     Function that gets the element at an index. Its type
                is void *(*)(void *collection, size_t index). It
                gives a pointer to the storage of the element. The
                sort reads and writes each element through such a
                pointer. The elements therefore do not have to be
                contiguous. A ring buffer, or any other storage with
                an index, is correct. Each pointer must stay valid
                for the full sort.
comparison_proc Function that compares two elements. Its type is
                int (*)(const void *, const void *). It gives a value
                less than 0, 0, or more than 0, as for qsort(3).
mprocs          Memory management procedures, or NULL for the default
                ones. An mprocs that is not NULL must hold all four of
                malloc, free, calloc and realloc.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Gives true on success. The trivial cases are also a success: a NULL
collection, a length of 0 or 1, and an elem_size of 0. None of them have
anything to do.
Gives false when length is more than the maximum element count of the
library. Gives false when mprocs is not NULL and does not hold all four
of malloc, free, calloc and realloc. Gives false when the internal
temporary merge buffer cannot be allocated. Gives false when
length * elem_size overflows size_t. The collection stays completely
unchanged in each of these cases.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A stable sort. It keeps the relative order of equal elements.
O(n log n) time complexity in every case
O(n) space complexity for the temporary merge buffer
An iterative algorithm, with no recursion

getter_proc and comparison_proc must both not be NULL when collection is
not NULL and length is 2 or more. This holds even when elem_size is 0,
although the macro then calls neither of them. Only a NULL collection, or
a length of 0 or 1, lets a call give no real procedures. A call that
breaks this rule asserts and aborts the process. It does not give false.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
// Sort a plain array
static void *int_at(void *col, size_t i) { return &((int *)col)[i]; }

static int int_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
  int x = *(const int *)a, y = *(const int *)b;
  return (x > y) - (x < y);
}

int arr[] = {3, 1, 2};
if (!csort_sort(arr, 3, sizeof(arr[0]), int_at, int_cmp, NULL)) {
  /* the sort failed: arr does not change */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR csort_get_default_comparison_proc (3),
.BR cvec_sort (3)
